  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmaeil Baghaei confirmed on 21 May that Tehran had received the latest US text and was "reviewing" it. Iran's semi-official ISNA described the proposal as having "narrowed the gaps to some extent" — a formulation without precedent in prior Iranian public statements on the negotiations, and consistent with an authorized trial balloon from pragmatist circles given ISNA's editorial orientation. Bloomberg reported simultaneously that two hard stops remain in place: Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei issued an internal directive that near-weapons-grade uranium stockpiles must not leave Iranian territory, while the US demands physical transfer of those stockpiles; and a separate dispute over Iran's demand for toll revenue from vessels transiting the Strait of Hormuz under any future arrangement remains unresolved. Pakistan's Interior Minister Mohsin Naqvi arrived in Tehran in an unannounced visit on 21-22 May as a special envoy carrying a US message. Pakistan's Army Chief Asim Munir postponed his own Tehran trip, conditioning travel on Iran accepting a "final formula." Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on the evening of 21 May there were "some good signs" but that Washington "has other options" if a deal cannot be concluded. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span> (p 0.25→0.22); supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Israeli lawmakers voted 110-0 in preliminary reading on 20 May in favor of a coalition-backed bill to dissolve the Knesset, with no members voting against. Hours later, a parallel dissolution bill sponsored by Benny Gantz passed preliminary reading 53-0. Neither bill specifies an election date. Israeli parliamentary procedure requires three further readings plus committee review before a bill becomes a statute. Netanyahu separately attempted on 20 May to reintroduce the haredi draft-exemption bill in the Knesset Foreign Affairs and Defense Committee as a retention mechanism; Degel HaTorah's spiritual leader Rabbi Dov Lando publicly instructed lawmakers to "not get drawn into political games and to support the dissolution of the Knesset." Finance Minister Smotrich remained within the coalition and made no formal departure announcement. Israel Democracy Institute analyst Assaf Shapira assessed September or October 2026 as the most probable election months, with August ruled out by the 90-day minimum procedural window. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (p 0.22→0.38); supports <span class="cite">F5</span> without move (Sunday cadence)</p>

      <p>The 45-day ceasefire extension announced by the US State Department on 15 May remains the governing framework as of EOD 22 May. State Department spokesperson Tommy Pigott confirmed the extension on 15 May; neither the US nor the Lebanese government issued a withdrawal of support in the 20-22 May window. IDF conducted daily airstrikes against Hezbollah infrastructure in southern Lebanon — activity explicitly permitted under the ceasefire's carve-out for planned, imminent, or ongoing attacks — but no strikes were recorded in Beirut's governorate. Hezbollah did not conduct a rocket barrage above the 50/day threshold. The IDF chief's characterization that there is effectively "no ceasefire" in south Lebanon is a description of conditions, not a formal government declaration of collapse. A Pentagon military coordination meeting between Israeli, Lebanese, and American military delegations is scheduled for 29 May; the fourth political round is set for 2-3 June in Washington. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> (RESOLVED-NO)</p>

      <p>Hormuz Strait transit data for 20-22 May shows 2-13 independently verified commercial vessels per day against a pre-crisis baseline of approximately 95-120 per day. IMF PortWatch recorded 2 commercial transits on 21 May. Iranian Navy sources claimed 31 vessels transiting on 22 May, but that count includes IRGC-escorted tankers excluded under the active forecast's ambiguity rule. Lloyd's war-risk premiums remain at approximately 8 times pre-crisis levels, with six P&amp;I clubs having withdrawn cover entirely. Maersk and MSC have not announced a return to Hormuz transits. Polymarket's "Hormuz normal by end of May" contract traded at 5% probability. Brent crude was reported at approximately $116.73 on 21 May, sustained by the unresolved strait situation. No insurance-market re-entry was observed in the 20-22 May window. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (p 0.38→0.12)</p>

      <p>Finance Minister Bezalel Smotrich held a press conference on 19 May confirming he had been informed of a secret ICC arrest warrant application against him related to West Bank settlement expansion; Smotrich described the move as "a declaration of war" and subsequently signed an evacuation order for the Palestinian Bedouin community of Khan al-Ahmar. Smotrich did not announce a formal coalition departure in this window. Avigdor Liberman issued a statement declaring his goal in the upcoming election is to become prime minister, a signal of full electoral mobilization. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span> without move (Sunday cadence)</p>

    </div>
  </div>

