  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>IRNA, Iran's official state news agency, ran Foreign Ministry spokesperson Baghaei's confirmation that Tehran had received the latest US text and was "reviewing" it. IRNA's framing throughout positioned Iran as conducting deliberate evaluation of American overtures from a posture of sovereignty rather than urgency. Each instance of Iranian flexibility in IRNA's coverage was paired with affirmations of "national interests," consistent with weeks-long editorial positioning aimed at domestic audiences. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>The semi-official ISNA described the latest US proposal as having "narrowed the gaps to some extent" — a carefully calibrated formulation that constitutes the most constructive public signal from an Iranian-linked outlet since negotiations began. ISNA's proximity to reformist and pragmatist government circles makes this a plausible authorized trial balloon from within the foreign policy apparatus, rather than an editorial slip. The formulation stops well short of imminent agreement and does not address the specific hard stops — uranium disposition and Hormuz toll demands — that Bloomberg confirmed remain unresolved. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Tasnim News Agency, affiliated with the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC), ran directly contradictory messaging: Tasnim explicitly rejected any suggestion that Iran's counterproposals addressed the disposition of nuclear materials or enrichment activities, constituting a direct pushback against Western media interpretation of Iranian flexibility. Tasnim's framing positioned the IRGC's red lines as non-negotiable and categorically distinct from whatever the Foreign Ministry is authorized to communicate through diplomatic channels. State broadcaster IRIB described previous US proposals as equivalent to demanding Iran's "surrender to Trump's excessive demands," framing unchanged from prior weeks. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan, the hardline daily whose editor Hossein Shariatmadari is a Khamenei appointee, continued to attribute domestic economic hardship to "enemy infiltration" rather than the Hormuz blockade — maintaining a siege-mentality editorial line consistent with its weeks-long positioning. Fars News, also IRGC-adjacent, continued to minimize the scale of ongoing negotiations. The divergence between ISNA's constructive framing and Tasnim/IRIB/Kayhan's resistance framing is consistent with a deliberate dual-track information posture: the government preserves diplomatic maneuverability through authorized pragmatist outlets while hardline outlets maintain domestic legitimacy for any eventual rejection of terms. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

